From 561230bc2cb183b4c2beca58b3597971e53d28a4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 11:21:58 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] fix(state): annotation watch read state.db/-shm with
 open()/close(), cancelling SQLite's locks

Root cause of an example gateway's repeated state.db index corruption.

kanban_annotation_watch._head() read the
database header and the WAL-index header of every watched state.db with
`with open(path, "rb")` every ~2 s in the Desktop backend (the kanban-annotation
lease holder). POSIX cancels every lock a process holds on a file when it closes ANY
descriptor for it, so the backend's SQLite connections ran without their WAL writer
lock and read marks (in an example reproduction: /proc/locks showed no POSIX lock for the
backend on state.db-shm while it held -shm open; an audit hook traced the opens to
_head). The backend and the gateway then wrote the same WAL at once: index entries
of recent rows were lost on every messages/sessions index, and the
gateway later hit SQLITE_CORRUPT and quarantined itself.

- _head reads through sqlite_safe_read.pread_lock_safe (cached descriptor, never
  closed); a missing file is still None and any other failure still raises.
- backup_sqlite._uses_wal read the header with open() while _safe_copy_db already
  held a connection to it (inside the gateway/CLI): same fix, stdlib fallback marked.
- scripts/check_sqlite_sidecar_opens.py missed both because the path reached open()
  as a parameter: a same-file function that passes a parameter to a file call
  (directly or through another such function, self./cls. methods included) is now a
  file call, flagged at the call site; marked calls do not propagate.

Tests: a foreign writer must stay locked out of a write transaction open in this
process across signature() (fails on the old code: the foreign writer acquires);
_uses_wal never calls the builtin open(); checker cases for helpers and wrappers.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
